ys and weeks, not <24 hours)", "Chronic Progressive Multisystem Syndrome: Weight loss and neurological changes do not clearly follow a chronic-progressive trajectory yet", "Isolated Hematological Malignancy Syndrome: Neurological symptoms (vision changes, ataxia) necessitate inclusion of neurological differential mechanisms"], "alarm_features": ["Constitutional symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss)", "35% blasts on CBC (strong alarm for hematological malignancy)", "Pancytopenia (anemia, thrombocytopenia, leukocytosis with blasts)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.004, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Non-focal neurological findings don't strongly argue against myeloid neoplasm (B1) since blasts can cause diffuse effects."]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.031, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of lymphadenopathy/hepatosplenomegaly weakens lymphoid neoplasm (B2) but doesn't rule it out."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.523171213823373, "posterior": 0.5869485000481579, "danger": 0.65, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Subacute presentation and lack of chronic disease history make blast crisis (B3) less likely."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "urgency", "representative_diseases": ["chronic myeloid leukemia", "polycythemia vera", "essential thrombocythemia", "primary myelofibrosis"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.256, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.123, "danger": 0.2, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": ["B3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "summary": "No direct evidence contradicts B1, though nonfocal neurological exam with ataxia suggests non-malignant mechanisms, but this is not the strongest finding."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the 35% blasts with pancytopenia and constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss) strongly support myeloid neoplasm (B1) over chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm (B3)?", "summary": "The high blast percentage and constitutional symptoms strongly favor myeloid neoplasm (B1) over chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm (B3)."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the pancytopenia and 35% blasts in peripheral blood consistent with chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm (B3) in blast crisis?", "summary": "Pancytopenia and blasts could occur in blast crisis but lack supporting chronic-phase features (e.g., prior splenomegaly)."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the nonfocal neurological exam with ataxia but no focal deficits inconsistent with myeloid neoplasm (B1) as the primary explanation?", "summary": "Non-focal neurological findings don't strongly argue against myeloid neoplasm (B1) since blasts can cause diffuse effects."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the absence of lymphadenopathy or hepatosplenomegaly in the physical exam inconsistent with lymphoid blast proliferation (B2)?", "summary